<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-23" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Lorentz Transformations</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Two people are somehow running along a straight track at relativistic
            speeds.
            Runner 1 is moving to the right at speed <m>\beta_{1}</m> relative to you,
            and runner 2 is moving to the left at speed <m>\beta_{2}</m> relative to
            you.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the relative speed you measure between these two observers?
                    This is, of course, something you do referencing the lattice clocks,
                    so be precise about what this means and calculate it.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    At what speed does one runner see the other one running towards
                    them?
                    Check that the <alert>speed</alert> of runner 1 relative to runner 2
                    is the same as the speed of runner 2 relative to runner 1.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        Consider the following situation, partially depicted below.
        You have a 25m long pole, and on campus there is a 15m long barn.
        Since you can run at speed 0.8c, one of your friends says: <q>If you run
        towards the barn, because of Lorentz contraction, I should be able to close
        the door and have the pole completely enclosed in the barn</q>.
    </p>
    <p>
        You disagree: <q>In my reference frame, the barn is 9m long and I'm just
        carrying a 25m long pole, so the pole will not fit in the barn.</q> This seems
        like a paradox: either the pole fits in the barn or it doesn't.
    </p>


    <image width="50%" source="images\activities\lect23\barn_and_pole.png"/>


    <p>
        Your task is as follows:
    </p>

    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Identify the root of the problem.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Figure out if someone is right or wrong
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Starting from reasoning that is solid (either from you or your
                    friend), use the Lorentz transformations to explain what the
                    other person sees.
                    You might be really worried about the ladder crashing into the
                    back door of the barn.
                    If you are, consider modifying the situation slightly so this is
                    no longer an issue (it might help with step 1).
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
        Note: this problem will reappear in the homework, so you will have more time
        to think about it.
    </p>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
